# USE — How to run a program

File: wiki/Use.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://wiki.openooda.org/#Use.oot

## 1. What you need

You need a checkout of the `ooda` tree that already has a seed compiler.
You need host `gcc`.

There is no working public one-line install today.
The line `curl | sh` against the public site is documented. It 404s for the public.
The compiler repo is private. The script pins an old tag and does not check a hash.
Do not pipe that script and expect a compiler.

Do not run `cargo`. That toolchain is gone.

## 2. Build the compiler from the checkout

From the `ooda` tree:

```
cd ooda
OODAC_BIN=./bootstrap/seed/oodac bash bootstrap/oodac_pure_build oodac/main.oo oodac/oodac
./oodac/oodac check oodac/main.oo
```

`bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` is the official rebuild.
It starts `gcc` and the seed compiler.
That is the one host shell entry. After that, you use `ooda` and `oodac`.

Put `./bin` on your `PATH` if you want the short `ooda` command:

```
export PATH="$PWD/bin:$PATH"
ooda version
```

## 3. Write a file

Save this as `hello.oo`:

```
pub fn greet(name: String) -> String {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

verify greet {
    assert_eq!(greet("World"), "Hello, World!");
}

pub fn main() {
    println(greet("openOODA"));
}
```

The checkout also has `fixtures/hello.oo` if you want a file that already exists.

## 4. Check, run, test

Typecheck only:

```
ooda check hello.oo
```

Typecheck, emit C, compile with `gcc`, run:

```
ooda run hello.oo
```

That prints the greeting. It does not run the `verify` block.

Run `verify` and asserts:

```
ooda test hello.oo
```

`ooda test` sets `OODA_TEST=1`. A failing assert fails the command.

JSON errors for an editor or a script:

```
ooda check hello.oo --json-errors
```

## 5. Fix a file the compiler can patch

`ooda fix` can apply a few error classes: missing import, missing `}`, type errors, unused `Result`, and a discarded `Result` wrap.

It asks yes or no before it writes, unless you pass `--yes`.
Writes go through `write_file`. That needs `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
If that env is unset, the write is denied.
No TTY and no `--yes` refuses.
Absolute paths and symlinks are refused.

```
export OODA_FS_WRITEDIR="$PWD"
ooda fix hello.oo --yes
```

